course: dictionary
lesson: 5054
title: Insatiable: never satisfied
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: Insatiable means always wanting more and never being satisfied. Look at the start of the word: in- means not, and satisfied means full up, so an insatiable person is never full up. «An insatiable appetite» means you can always eat more. «An insatiable curiosity» means you always want to know more.
explain: Kipling's Elephant's Child is «full of 'satiable curtiosity». This is his playful baby-talk for insatiable curiosity. He asks so many questions that he goes to the great grey-green, greasy Limpopo River to find out what the Crocodile has for dinner.
explain: The word is an adjective, so it describes a noun. Check the clues around it: always, never, more, and so many questions. Those words show you the meaning, even if you have never seen insatiable before.
fact: Insatiable means always wanting more and never satisfied.
fact: «Insatiable» is an adjective, so it describes a noun: an insatiable appetite, an insatiable curiosity.
fact: «'Satiable curtiosity» is Kipling's baby-talk for «insatiable curiosity».
fact: Kipling spelt «curtiosity» on purpose, to sound like a small child.
fact: Stories and science also use the word, for example an insatiable predator that is always hunting for more food.
exercise: «The puppy had an insatiable appetite and asked for a second dinner.» What does insatiable mean here? | *Always wanting more | Very tired | Quiet and calm | Scared of dinner
exercise: Kipling's Elephant's Child is «full of 'satiable curtiosity». What is this baby-talk short for? | curious crocodile | *insatiable curiosity | satisfied curiosity | insatiable appetite
exercise: «Ben had an insatiable appetite, and he asked for a second helping, then a third.» Which clue shows that insatiable means always wanting more? | an appetite | asked | Ben | *a second helping, then a third
test: «She has an insatiable thirst for adventure.» What does insatiable mean? | Bored of adventure | *Always wanting more and never satisfied | Thirsty only at lunchtime | Afraid of the dark
test: Why did Kipling write «curtiosity» instead of «curiosity»? | To show he is a crocodile | To make the word longer | By accident, with no reason at all | *To sound like a small child using baby-talk
exam: «The insatiable explorer read every map, asked every question and still wanted more.» What does insatiable mean here? | *Never satisfied, always wanting more | Lost on the map | Tired from walking | Happy to stop
